// 34 — `::CORBA::Principal` is a name the front end predeclares and the wire
// does not carry, and until 2026-08-25 the registry called it **`void`**.
//
// This is the shape of the defect, not a description of it: `struct S {
// ::CORBA::Principal who; long n; }` was accepted by `sidl-validate` with two
// unrelated advisories, and both emitters produced a member that marshals
// **zero bytes** — `("who", "who", "void")` in Python, `pub who: ()` in Rust.
// A peer that writes a Principal there hands us nothing and every field after
// it is mis-parsed. Nothing was red.
//
// It is the same defect that had already been fixed for the sibling name.
// `sema.rs` predeclares four names inside module `CORBA`, and the registry
// answered exactly one of them — `TypeCode` — because the fix was scoped to
// the keyword that reported it rather than to the table it lived in.
// `Principal` is the next line of that table. The rule now has a home
// (`orbweaver_idl::sema::PREDECLARED_CORBA`) and a sweep over it
// (`orbweaver-registry`'s
// `every_predeclared_corba_name_reaches_a_typecode_or_is_refused`): every
// predeclared name reaches a `TypeCode` the registry means, or is refused.
// The other two — `Object` and `ValueBase` — are refused as scoped names,
// because `object` and `valuebase` are IDL keywords and cannot be the tail of
// one; their keyword spellings are covered by `corpus/golden/16` and `32`.
//
// Golden rather than negative because the *oracle accepts the file*, which is
// what decided the repair. `omniidl -b dump` on this file, omniORB 4.3.4,
// measured 2026-08-25, exits 0 and prints the member with its type intact:
//
//     module gp34 {
//
//       struct Envelope {
//         CORBA::Principal sender;
//         string routing;
//       };
//       ...
//
// So refusing the name in the front end would be a divergence from a
// conformant compiler over a file it parses, and a `corpus/divergences.tsv`
// row for no gain. `Principal` was withdrawn from CORBA — it left the GIOP
// request header after 1.0 and CORBA 3.x removed it — and "this version
// marshals no value for it" is a *wire* answer, which is where it is now
// given: the registry records `TypeCode::Principal`, `orbweaver-dynamic`
// refuses it by name, and both emitters skip the declaration rather than
// serving a member that writes nothing.
//
// **The fifth family landed 2026-08-26** and this paragraph is what commissioned
// it, so it says what changed rather than being deleted. It used to read: *"S4
// does not warn on it the way it warns on a native or a valuetype. Making it a
// fifth family means one published refusal head in `orbweaver-dynamic` and both
// emitters using it… three crates, one change."* That is what was done —
// `orbweaver_dynamic::withdrawn_wire_head` is the head, both emitters call it
// through `withdrawn_principal`, and this file's five unservable declarations
// are now five `wire/deferred-type` warnings with a position and a fix.
//
// What the paragraph got wrong is worth more than what it got right. It scoped
// the work to S4 and the emitters, and the batch that did it found **two live
// falsehoods in shipped code** that nobody was looking for: `from_json`
// answered a peer `"principal cannot cross yet"` and `decode` answered
// `"cannot decode principal yet"`. "Yet" promises a release in which the type
// comes back; CORBA 3.0 removed it. Those are the same two sentences the
// `native` family was repaired for on 2026-08-21, in the same two layers, over
// a different `TypeCode` — and they survived that sweep because a sweep scoped
// to a keyword fixes a keyword. Three gates were green over this file while
// they were live, and `deferred_wire_agreement`'s two sets agreed **because
// both were empty**: the rule named nothing here, the emitters' catch-all
// sentences carried no published head, and the filter that reads the heads
// could not see them.
//
// The wording chosen for the fifth head says withdrawal rather than deferral or
// absence, because those are three different instructions to a contract author,
// and this family is the only one whose fix can name a *replacement*: caller
// identity moved into a CSIv2 service context (`orbweaver_giop::csiv2`'s
// `IdentityToken`), so S4 says where it went instead of only that it left.
module gp34 {
  // ── unservable: reaches ::CORBA::Principal ──────────────────────────────
  // as a member, which is the shape that produced the `void`
  struct Envelope { ::CORBA::Principal sender; string routing; };
  // as a return type and as a parameter
  interface Gateway {
    ::CORBA::Principal whoami();
    void stamp(in ::CORBA::Principal caller);
  };
  // through a sequence element
  typedef sequence<::CORBA::Principal> Roll;
  // through a typedef of the name itself
  typedef ::CORBA::Principal Caller;
  // one hop further out, so the cascade is executed and not just the direct case
  struct Manifest { Envelope sealed; long weight; };

  // ── servable: the negative controls ─────────────────────────────────────
  // The sibling predeclared name is servable and must stay so: it is
  // `tk_TypeCode`, and this file would pass just as well if the fix had
  // broken it.
  struct Described { ::CORBA::TypeCode what; string label; };
  // A reference to an unservable interface is still an IOR.
  struct Desk { Gateway agent; };
  struct Plain { long a; string b; };
  interface Lookup { Plain find(in string key); };
};
